# .rooignore — what the AGENT must not read into its context.
#
# NOT the same job as .gitignore. .gitignore protects the REPOSITORY from
# files that should never be committed. This protects the CONVERSATION from
# files that should never be read: raw business data, database snapshots, and
# large generated output.
#
# CONFIDENTIALITY FIRST, COST SECOND. `data-exploration` already forbids
# PASTING identifying rows into insights.md. Nothing until wave 33 prevented
# the agent from READING the whole corpus into context in the first place -
# and a rule about what may be written down does not cover what has already
# been looked at.
#
# THIS DOES NOT CONTRADICT THE COMPATIBILITY RECORD'S REJECTION OF
# .rooignore. That rejection concerns placing one inside somebody ELSE'S
# external project, which is a modification of their repository. This is the
# framework's own project hiding its own raw data from its own agent.
# Different repository, different purpose. There is no contradiction to
# resolve.
#
# FIXTURES STAY READABLE. tests/fixtures/ is sanitised structure by rule, it
# is what the agent must read to write a reader, and excluding it would break
# the build loop.

# local environment and build output
.venv/
__pycache__/
dist/
build/

# real business files used while building
data/

# credentials
.env

# machine evidence receipts (wave 38): one JSON file per run, and a long
# build makes many. The agent cites them BY NAME from memory.md; it has no
# reason to read the whole folder into context.
.evidence/

# station exhaust: may hold large raw query output
skills_output/**/artifacts/

# large binaries the agent should never read whole
*.parquet
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
